- op: discuss  # 2d94
  at: '2026-09-22T05:35:31Z'  # 2d94
  by: agent  # 2d94
  actor: jgstern_agent  # 2d94
  clock: 5  # 2d94
  nonce: 2d94  # 2d94
  message: "DONE, merged to dev. python's LanguageDdgSpec now supplies name_for and kind_for, derived from py.py's actual naming rule rather than assumed.\n\nTHE RULE, probed over all four shapes: top / top.inner / Outer.meth / Inner.deep -- prefix with the IMMEDIATE enclosing scope, one level, class OR function; kind is 'method' exactly when that scope is a class. NESTED FUNCTIONS WERE MIS-KEYED TOO, which this row did not notice.\n\nMEASURED on this repository:\n  methods         0 -> 21,275 of 24,941  (85.3%)   <- the exact share this row predicted\n  functions      69.1% -> 71.0%\n  all callables  ~21.7% -> 80.8%\n  orphan ddg keys ~21,600 -> 145\n\nNOT MERELY A RE-LABELLING. The §3a walk census on this repo against its own claims, same binary otherwise: TOTAL WALKS 84 -> 122 (+45%). The walk genuinely runs where it structurally could not.\n\nBUT THE DIRECTION IS THE ONE THIS ROW WARNED ABOUT, and I am recording it rather than burying it: of the +38 new walks, +32 ESCAPE (result None) and only +6 establish dependence.\n\nTHE A/B THIS ROW DEMANDED IS NOT SATISFIED. Pre-registered refutation, written before measuring: 'if ADR-0046 useful precision DROPS, the fix is a reporting regression even though the lookup is correct, and should not ship as-is.' It could NOT be tested this session -- this repo's claims are all repaired and report zero violating flows by construction; prospector under docs/example-claims/generic-taint-claims.yaml yields zero evidence; measure-taint-precision.py requires per-flow human scoring. So: mechanism fixed and measured, precision effect UNMEASURED. A finding leaving the structurally-identified class enters the useful-precision denominator, so the risk to the published 30.9% headline is real and open. Filed as a residual.\n\nShipped on the owner's instruction to tackle this row, with the limitation stated in the commit, the changelog and here.\n\nFAMILY, now enumerated: go supplies both hooks; rust omits them WITH A WRITTEN REASON at rust_def_use.py:357 (its methods are function_item nested in impl_item, so the defaults are already correct -- a reasoned absence, not this defect); python is fixed here; BOTH ts_def_use registrations omit them with no reason."  # 2d94
